  ],
  "rationale": "**Does not hold: a dependency's embedded plugin activates without consent when a `[[plugins]]` chained reference names that dependency.**\n\nEvery facet resolves over `plugins::active_plugins`: skills and MCP servers at sync, and in `hook.rs` `prewarm_hook_sources`, `discovery_hint` and `dispatch_plugin_hooks`. That function has two ways to get cargo ids onto its worklist:\n\n1. **Consent seeding (holds).** `discovery::enabled_dependencies` adds a workspace dependency only when `[plugins] auto-enable` or an applicable `use` entry covers it and `disable` does not (`!is_disabled && (is_auto_enabled || is_used)`). The other ids it adds are `use`d names, so they need a `use` entry too. `discover`/`decide` only classify dependency offers (an undecided one is `Candidate`) and write nothing. Registry and workspace plugins are trust roots loaded by `load_registry_impl`, so they are not dependency plugins.\n2. **Chained edges (violates).** For every active plugin, `record_active` pushes `CargoPm::id_for(&edge.name, None)` for each `[[plugins]] source.cargo` edge whose predicates hold. Nothing checks `[plugins]` for these ids. The fixed-point then calls `pms.load_plugin(&id)`, which loads the crate's embedded `Symposium.toml` / `[package.metadata.symposium]` / `skills/` and records it as active. So if a registry plugin (or a workspace plugin, or a consented crate plugin) chains to a workspace dependency, that dependency's plugin activates even though neither `auto-enable` nor `use` names it.\n\nConcrete case, with a passing test: `tests/init_sync.rs::sync_installs_skill_via_crate_manifest` (fixture `crate-manifest0`). The workspace path-depends on `crate-m`, the user registry's `vouch-m` chains `source.cargo = \"crate-m\"`, and `config.toml` has no `[plugins]` section. `sync` installs `crate-m`'s `m-guidance` skill. The same path dispatches chained crates' hooks, MCP servers and subcommands (`hook_fires_from_chained_crate`, `sync_registers_mcp_server_from_chained_crate`, `dispatches_subcommand_from_chained_crate`). This may be intended, with the chaining trust root vouching for the crate, but the requirement as written has no chain exception. Resolve by adding the exception to the text, or by gating chained ids on `[plugins]`. This is the known chain gap shared with `consent.disable_wins`.\n\nWhat does hold for the direct consent path (tests pass): `tests/enablement.rs::use_records_workspace_entry_and_installs` (with no consent, `sync` installs none of `crate-a`'s skills; after `use crate-a` they install), `consent_prompt_never_fires_non_interactively` (non-interactive `sync` records nothing, `crate-a` stays a pending candidate, and its skill is not installed), `discovery::tests::undecided_dependency_plugin_is_a_candidate`, and `enabled_dependencies_reads_config_not_offers`. Hook-path exclusion of unconsented crates is judged from code, because `dispatch_plugin_hooks` iterates the same `active_plugins` set. No test fires a hook with an unconsented dependency.",
  "requirement": "Symposium MUST NOT activate a plugin embedded in a workspace dependency unless `[plugins] auto-enable` or an applicable `[plugins] use` entry names that dependency."
}
